- How would NUMA-aware placements affect performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of memory layout, overlap handling, and cache-friendly copy strategies in tight, real-world paths.

## Key Concepts
- Overlap-safe memmove
- Cache-friendly 64-byte blocks
- Alignment assumptions and tail handling
- Fallback to memcpy for tiny or unaligned data
- Microbenchmark plan to measure throughput vs standard memcpy

## Code Example
```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void *memmove64(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n){
  unsigned char *d = (unsigned char*)dst;
  const unsigned char *s = (const unsigned char*)src;
  const size_t BLOCK = 64;

  if ((size_t)(d) <= (size_t)(s) || d >= s + n){
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      d += BLOCK; s += BLOCK; n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d, s, n);
  } else {
    d += n; s += n;
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      d -= BLOCK; s -= BLOCK;
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d - n, s - n, n);
  }
  return dst;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for arbitrary alignment and larger data sizes?
- Compare performance to builtin memmove on x86-64 with different runtimes.
- What edge cases or architectures could break this approach?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates a practical lock-free pattern for data reads that must remain consistent without heavy synchronization.

## Key Concepts
- sequence lock
- memory_order_acquire/release
- barrier considerations on x86 vs ARM

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t ver; Config cfg128; } SharedConfig;

void write_config(SharedConfig* sc, Config new_cfg) {
  __atomic_fetch_add(&sc->ver, 1, memory_order_acq_rel); // make odd
  sc->cfg128 = new_cfg;
  __atomic_fetch_add(&sc->ver, 1, memory_order_release); // make even
}

Config read_config(SharedConfig* sc) {
  Config c;
  uint64_t v;
  do {
    v = __atomic_load_n(&sc->ver, memory_order_acquire);
    if (v & 1) continue;
    c = sc->cfg128;
  } while (__atomic_load_n(&sc->ver, memory_order_acquire) != v);
  return c;
}
```

